WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.304
-Hey, everybody,
welcome to "Corrections"

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:08.174
for the week of May 24th.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.910
We changed the title card again.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.045
We've been trying to find
the right way to describe this,

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:16.982
and it is now
an online inclusive.

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:19.618
We were told, you know,
it's not really that exclusive.

00:00:19.618 --> 00:00:22.521
Anybody can watch it.
But it's very inclusive

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:25.625
in that we are responding
pretty much to every --

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:28.794
We've invited all of you
to participate.

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:31.130
You know, all you need
is an Internet connection

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.335
and a total lack of boundaries,
and here we are.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:38.371
Also, I want -- I like this.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:41.640
A lot of the comments, people
are saying, "Are you okay?

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.443
You seem aggressive."
I really genuinely like this.

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:46.312
I mean, the fact that
week in and week out,

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:49.048
I can make myself better
with your help

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:50.516
means a great deal to me.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.952
So I'm sorry if it ever
comes across that I am angry

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:57.824
in any way, shape, or form
in responding to your feedback.

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.425
So, last week, I talked
about the fact

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:03.530
that I sometimes say,
"That is the definition of."

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.231
And it was pointed out to me

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:08.400
that I should say,
"That is the epitome of."

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.539
And then I said
to make a correction like that

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:16.842
is the epitome of pedantic.

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:19.512
And then a lot of you said

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:23.283
it's actually
the epitome of pedantry,

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:25.018
'cause it has to be a noun.

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:30.623
And I just want
to say thank you.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:33.426
And also that I'm so impressed

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:37.530
you jackals can type in
with your little jackal paws.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.632
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.201
I mean --

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:46.438
I mean, to make a correction --

00:01:46.438 --> 00:01:48.675
to be called pedantic

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.844
and then say,
"It's actually pedantry,"

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:53.613
that would be like -- it'd be
like getting on an airplane,

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.581
a guy taking off his shoes
and putting his socks

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:57.650
on the tray table,
and you saying,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.451
"Hey, man, I don't want
to look at your socks."

00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:00.853
And then he just peels --

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:03.523
looks at you
and just peels the socks off.

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:10.429
Oh, I said we had
a crocodile smoke a vape pen.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:14.233
A lot of people said
you don't smoke a vape pen --

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:17.403
You vape a vape pen.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:22.275
Or to put it more succinctly,
you look ridiculous.

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.510
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.680
And, look, I'm just worried

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.184
that teens are going
to start vaping more

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:34.053
because of "Mare of Easttown."

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.728
'Cause everybody
wants to be Mare.

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:45.097
Solving murders,
vaping their vape pens,

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:47.467
and making eyes at Guy Pearce.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.406
This one, I...feel bad about.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:59.111
I said "shayman"
when it's shaman.

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:01.046
And what makes it worse

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:06.185
is my staff has tried to
correct me on this in the past,

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.855
and I continue to get it wrong.

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:10.890
So "sham" on me.
[shame/shaman]

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.861
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:17.797
That was a joke
somebody made in "Corrections."

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.099
And -- But because I'm a dad,

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:22.602
I'm legally allowed
to steal that joke.

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:29.908
I talked about
our photographer Lloyd,

00:03:29.908 --> 00:03:32.512
who's Scottish.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:36.282
And, you know,
I talked like him.

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:38.685
I used his voice,
and someone said,

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:43.256
"That Scottish accent
is an abomination,"

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.458
which is what we tell him
every day.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:48.194
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:51.330
Like, we know.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.200
'Cause I've heard -- I've heard
regular Scottish people,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.005
and Lloyd ain't that.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:00.139
You know, we'll say, "Lloyd,

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.008
can you get some pictures
of the guests?"

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:04.077
[ Scottish accent ]
"I don't have my camera!"

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.212
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.747
"Nobody told me!"

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.649
[ Normal voice ]
We're like, "Why do we --"

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:11.417
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.224
This might be a trap,
but I think it's true.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:21.694
I had multiple
Canadian viewers told me

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.996
that Canadians
don't say Toronto.

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.032
They say "Trono."

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:28.167
Like, you don't hear
the second "T."

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:31.570
And I believe
they were Canadians,

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.107
because they were very --

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:36.075
In their correction,
they were very apologetic

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.710
about even bringing it up.

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:43.649
I said Kroger's.

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:45.784
It's Kroger.

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:48.921
I said Kroger's on purpose

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.590
just to, like [bleep] with you.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.625
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.628 --> 00:05:00.733
Hey, you know, we did it
tonight in the show.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:03.035
I did Werner Herzog,

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:05.571
and a lot of people

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:08.441
said that my Werner Herzog
is Christoph Waltz.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.309
Nonsense.

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:12.111
You know,

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:15.147
look, it's not the best Werner
Herzog in the world, but

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.017
[as Herzog] Werner Herzog
would say of "Corrections,"

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:21.053
it is a man's
desperate attempt,

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:22.722
[Normal voice] whereas
Christoph Waltz would be like

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.525
[as Waltz] "Corrections"!
It's time to begin!

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:27.527
[ Normal voice ]
It's, like, enthusiasm.

00:05:27.527 --> 00:05:29.295
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:31.998
[ As Waltz ] Here we go!

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:33.298
Number one.

00:05:33.298 --> 00:05:35.001
[ Normal voice ] Um...

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:38.538
And other people

00:05:38.538 --> 00:05:40.772
said my Herzog
sounded like John Malkovich,

00:05:40.772 --> 00:05:43.041
which is equally
offensive to me,

00:05:43.041 --> 00:05:46.579
[as Malkovich]
because John Malkovich is here.

00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:48.514
It's up here.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:51.083
It's very different.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:54.086
And to say

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:58.858
that I sound like Werner Herzog

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:04.363
is an idea that I dae not
entertain, sir!

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:06.532
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:10.769
[ Normal voice ]
This isn't a correction.

00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:14.840
This is a-a story

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.508
about John Malkovich.

00:06:16.508 --> 00:06:19.612
So maybe could we --

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:23.716
Could we make a title card?

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:26.518
It will say, um...

00:06:26.518 --> 00:06:29.388
"Celebrity Anecdote
in the Body of 'Corrections.'"

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:31.891
And can we just make that
and add it in post?

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:35.260
So -- Thank you.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:38.664
So, John Malkovich hosts "SNL."

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:42.801
2008. And he was in a sketch --

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:44.136
There was a sketch
that was written

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:48.574
where it was a lost
Judy Blume novel,

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:51.209
and he was a character
in a Judy Blume novel.

00:06:51.209 --> 00:06:55.747
And his outfit was a pink
turtleneck and pink overalls.

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:58.550
And on Friday, he had to take
a photo shoot for it.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.118
And so he wore it
for the photo shoot,

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:01.520
and then he just
kept it on all day

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.689
because he said
it was super comfortable.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:06.058
So already greatest day
of my life.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:08.994
John Malkovich, spending time
with him, already the best,

00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:12.431
and he's wearing a pink
turtleneck and pink overalls.

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:15.101
And then I said to him,
"What did you do last night?"

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.536
And he said to me,

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:21.106
[as Malkovich] "I was supposed
to meet a friend for dinner.

00:07:21.106 --> 00:07:25.811
But they canceled,
and so I ate alone.

00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:30.249
And to spend --
To pass the time,

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:36.088
I tried to think of the name of
every film I'd ever appeared in.

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:40.660
And the 65th film I thought of

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.062
was 'Being John Malkovich.'"

00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:45.764
[ Laughter ]

00:07:45.764 --> 00:07:48.301
"And you'd think
it would have been earlier."

00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:53.272
[ Normal voice ] And then can we
just do a title card that says,

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.941
"This has been
Celebrity Anecdote

00:07:55.941 --> 00:07:58.311
in the Body of 'Corrections'"?

00:08:00.146 --> 00:08:04.349
And thank you for
turning those around so quickly.

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:05.617
Oh, this is embarrassing.

00:08:05.617 --> 00:08:08.020
We were talking about
how calculators --

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:13.425
Back in the day, on calculators,
you could spell out BOOBS

00:08:13.425 --> 00:08:17.896
if you typed 80085.

00:08:17.896 --> 00:08:21.133
A lot of people
pointed out to me

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:25.571
that what you would actually do
is 58008

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:28.374
and then turn it upside-down,

00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:31.810
'cause that was --
That was the reveal

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.113
that made it titillating.

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:35.481
You know, like, 'cause as
a teenage boy,

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:36.715
you'd be like,
"That's just numbers."

00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:38.818
And then you be like,
"Ah-oogah!"

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:40.919
[ Laughter ]

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.522
'Cause we didn't have
the Internet, fellas.

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:46.625
We had to -- We had to
get it where we could.

00:08:46.625 --> 00:08:48.694
[ Laughter ]

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:50.763
[ Laughs ]

00:08:50.763 --> 00:08:52.098
And if the teacher came over,

00:08:52.098 --> 00:08:53.365
and she's like,
"What are you boys doing?"

00:08:53.365 --> 00:08:55.701
we're like, "Just the 80,000s."

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:05.077
Um, oh, this is a correction
for a waiter I had last night.

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:09.548
So, last week --

00:09:09.548 --> 00:09:11.517
Was it last week that you were
on the show, Wally?

00:09:11.517 --> 00:09:13.285
-Yeah,
-So, last week,

00:09:13.285 --> 00:09:15.887
Action Bronson, who we love,
canceled.

00:09:15.887 --> 00:09:18.791
We did not have time.

00:09:18.791 --> 00:09:21.794
This makes it seem like,
"We didn't have time,

00:09:21.794 --> 00:09:23.429
so we had to --"
But we were very happy.

00:09:23.429 --> 00:09:25.931
We said, "You know what?"
'Cause it was very late.

00:09:25.931 --> 00:09:28.934
We said we'll have
Cue Card Wally as a guest.

00:09:28.934 --> 00:09:30.836
And it was genuinely great.

00:09:30.836 --> 00:09:33.172
And I got a ton of feedback
from people

00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:35.440
who enjoyed listening to Wally
tell his stories.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.376
And then last night,
I'm at dinner,

00:09:37.376 --> 00:09:40.212
and my waiter
walks over and said,

00:09:40.212 --> 00:09:43.716
"Hey, uh, tell Cue Card Willie
we love him here."

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:45.718
[ Laughter ]

00:09:48.353 --> 00:09:50.956
And I said,
"Uh, you bet I will."

00:09:50.956 --> 00:09:52.792
[ Laughter ]

00:09:55.695 --> 00:09:59.431
Mm, uh...

00:09:59.431 --> 00:10:04.069
Oh, this is
a very similar criticism,

00:10:04.069 --> 00:10:06.339
that Mikey the Shoe

00:10:06.339 --> 00:10:09.108
sounds like Tony the Fauch,

00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:12.544
which is Tony Fauci,
Anthony Fauci, Dr. Fauci,

00:10:12.544 --> 00:10:14.347
'cause he's from Brooklyn.

00:10:14.347 --> 00:10:15.514
But they're very different.

00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:18.417
Tony the Fauch is,
first of all, beloved.

00:10:18.417 --> 00:10:21.653
[ Laughter ]

00:10:21.653 --> 00:10:23.322
So when he goes back
to Brooklyn, he's like,

00:10:23.322 --> 00:10:26.725
"Hey, Tony the Fauch is --
It's the summer of the Fauch!"

00:10:26.725 --> 00:10:28.994
And people are like, "Hey, Tony,
can I take my mask off?"

00:10:28.994 --> 00:10:31.897
He's like, "You could
take it all off for all I care!"

00:10:31.897 --> 00:10:34.533
[ Laughter ]

00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:36.135
They'd be like,
"Tony, give me a number."

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:38.804
"You want my number? 58008,

00:10:38.804 --> 00:10:40.606
upside-down!"

00:10:40.606 --> 00:10:44.410
[ Laughter ]

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:45.711
Whereas Mikey the Shoe,

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:49.114
you know, he's from --
he's from the Bronx.

00:10:49.114 --> 00:10:52.884
He goes back there,
they're like, "Get outta here!"

00:10:52.884 --> 00:10:54.486
He's like, "Oh!

00:10:54.486 --> 00:10:56.689
You're just mad at me
'cause I made it out."

00:10:56.689 --> 00:10:59.925
They're like,
"You still live here."

00:10:59.925 --> 00:11:02.228
He's like, "Yeah,
but I went to Manhattan today,

00:11:02.228 --> 00:11:03.629
and then I came home,"
and they're like,

00:11:03.629 --> 00:11:05.898
"That's not making --
You commute."

00:11:05.898 --> 00:11:07.700
[ Laughter ]

00:11:11.069 --> 00:11:15.808
Somebody said to me...

00:11:15.808 --> 00:11:18.443
that I said, "This is
a perfect quote."

00:11:18.443 --> 00:11:21.413
And quote is the verb,
and quotation --

00:11:21.413 --> 00:11:24.250
I should say, "This is
the perfect quotation."

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:28.454
I looked it up, and it turns out
the way I did it is also fine.

00:11:28.454 --> 00:11:31.556
So that's not necessarily
a correction.

00:11:31.556 --> 00:11:34.427
That's just an example

00:11:34.427 --> 00:11:36.762
of the [bleep]
I have to put up with.

00:11:36.762 --> 00:11:40.332
[ Laughter ]

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:42.133
Another thing is, people said,
"These don't --

00:11:42.133 --> 00:11:44.002
Sometimes these don't
feel like corrections."

00:11:44.002 --> 00:11:46.071
Someone said, "You know,

00:11:46.071 --> 00:11:50.375
you should start calling it
'Justifications.'"

00:11:50.375 --> 00:11:52.911
And I'm going to keep
calling it "Corrections,"

00:11:52.911 --> 00:11:57.449
but you can call it whatever --
whatever you want.

00:11:57.449 --> 00:12:01.086
Um, guys, I like this.

00:12:01.086 --> 00:12:03.589
This makes me happy. And I --

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:07.659
Again, I feel like
I drifted away

00:12:07.659 --> 00:12:10.996
from the joy part, but...

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:15.734
we're about to go
on a week's hiatus,

00:12:15.734 --> 00:12:18.036
and, uh,

00:12:18.036 --> 00:12:22.741
I just want
to let all of you know

00:12:22.741 --> 00:12:27.113
that I will spend that time
with my wife.

00:12:29.481 --> 00:12:31.917
And there will be corrections
every day.

